  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1059 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nA damaging thunderstorm complex is ongoing across southern\nNebraska and northern Kansas late this evening. High cirrus from\nthis system 200 miles to our west may partially obscure \ntonight's lunar eclipse. Beyond this, there is a low chance for \nshowers or an isolated thunderstorm tonight, but most remain \ndry. Temperatures fall into the low 60s by Friday morning with \nquiet weather conditions. Our next weather system begins to take\nshape to our west during Friday afternoon. This will cause \nsouth winds to slowly increase throughout the day as \ntemperatures warm into the mid 80s. A shortwave trough glances \nthe area to the north late Friday night into early Saturday. The\nbetter thunderstorm chances remain focused north of the area, \nbut scattered showers or thunderstorms may dip into northeast \nNebraska and western Iowa as this system passes.\n\nThis weekend sees temperatures build. A combination of the\nSouthwest ridge and troughing to the north will not only fuel\nwarmer temperatures, but also result in breezy conditions. It is\nexpected that temperatures on Saturday and Sunday push into the\nlow to mid 90s across most of the area. It will be humid as\nwell, resulting in heat index values approaching 100. With any \nweather disturbances passing far to the north of the area and \nhot temperatures in place, thunderstorm chances are unlikely. \nEven if thunderstorms approach the area, increasing stability \nunder the ridge should limit their persistence. \n\nHeading into next week, the ridge will begin to flatten. This\nhelps lower temperatures back into the upper 80s to low 90s,\nthough this remains above normal. With more zonal upper-level\nwinds returning to the northern Plains, periodic thunderstorm\nchances return to the forecast. The best chance for\nthunderstorms will primarily be focused during the evening and\novernight hours.",
